Stress Urinary Incontinence
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Leakage with increased intra-abdominal pressure, Provoked by coughing/sneezing, Absence of urinary urgency, Positive cough stress test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-043
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of urinary leakage secondary to a violent coughing fit, resulting in profound distress, skin excoriation, and a fear of total bladder failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing continuous, high-volume urinary leakage during a severe upper respiratory tract infection, causing acute painful vulvovaginal excoriation and severe distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute overflow incontinence secondary to retention, assess the skin integrity, and provide immediate pharmacological and barrier management before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have a terrible cough, and every single time I cough, I completely wet myself. My skin is raw and burning from the constant urine.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of involuntary leakage of small volumes of urine strictly associated with increased intra-abdominal pressure (laughing, lifting). Over the past 48 hours, they developed a severe viral upper respiratory tract infection with a violent, paroxysmal cough. This has provoked constant, high-volume urine leakage with every coughing fit. They explicitly state a complete absence of sudden compelling urinary urgency preceding the leakage. There is a lack of dysuria, haematuria, or severe nocturia. The patient has a history of three vaginal deliveries with macrosomic infants. The constant leakage tonight has caused severe, burning pain in the vulvar and perineal skin.
Functional Status: Ambulation is preserved, but the patient is restricting all movement to avoid triggering a cough and subsequent leakage.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe embarrassment and distress; painful impaired skin integrity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.3C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, holding a towel, coughing frequently during the consultation.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Soft, non-tender; complete absence of a palpable, distended bladder, ruling out overflow retention. Genitourinary: Direct visualization confirms immediate, involuntary spurts of urine leakage from the urethral meatus perfectly synchronized with a provoked cough test. The vaginal mucosa is pale and thin, consistent with atrophic vaginitis in this postmenopausal patient. Severe, erythematous contact dermatitis (urine excoriation) is visible on the perineum and medial thighs. Expected investigation results will show a normal midstream urine culture, excluding infection, accompanied by objective demonstration of stress-induced leakage without concurrent detrusor overactivity on formal urodynamic testing.
Environmental Observations: Multiple soiled towels and inadequate, saturated sanitary pads in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter cough suppressants with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Stress Urinary Incontinence - Status: Exacerbated (Secondary to Acute Cough)
Differentials:

Overactive Bladder (Urge Incontinence) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit absence of sudden, compelling sensory urgency preceding the void; the leakage is purely mechanical and synchronous with the cough.

Overflow Incontinence (Retention) - Ruled out/unlikely as the bladder is not palpable or dull to percussion, indicating it is adequately emptying rather than passively overflowing.

Acute Bacterial Cystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of dysuria, frequency, and a negative point-of-care urinalysis for nitrites.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute mechanical crisis of stress urinary incontinence, massively exacerbated by the violently increased intra-abdominal pressure from an acute coughing illness. The constant leakage has caused severe, painful moisture-associated skin damage (excoriation). An urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute urinary retention (overflow), assess and immediately treat the severe skin breakdown, and prescribe a powerful antitussive to halt the mechanical trigger causing the incontinence. Deferring care would have resulted in worsening, agonizing skin excoriation and unmanageable distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral Pholcodine 10mg every 6 hours to aggressively suppress the severe paroxysmal cough triggering the leakage. Instructed the application of a thick zinc oxide barrier cream (e.g., Sudocrem) to the excoriated perineum. Recommended the immediate use of highly absorbent, purpose-designed incontinence pants rather than standard sanitary pads.
Positive Behavioral Change: To protect the compromised skin barrier, the patient is instructed to apply the thick zinc barrier cream immediately after gently patting the area dry with a dedicated soft towel, rather than wiping. Eliminating the sharp, burning pain of urine hitting raw skin provides immediate positive reinforcement, ensuring they build the habit of consistently maintaining the protective ointment layer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): High-capacity pull-up incontinence garments advised.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week to assess resolution of the viral cough, healing of the skin, and to discuss long-term management of the stress incontinence (e.g., pelvic floor physiotherapy, topical estrogen).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review is required if the patient develops a high fever, cloudy or foul-smelling urine, or if the skin breakdown begins weeping yellow pus, indicating a secondary bacterial skin infection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe leakage is a temporary mechanical consequence of the violent cough combined with a weakened pelvic floor; it will improve as the cough settles.
Clinical Course: The cough suppressant will reduce the physical pressure on the bladder. The barrier cream will soothe the burning skin within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop a fever, burning when you actually pee, or if the red skin becomes hot and starts oozing pus, you must seek medical care to rule out a bacterial infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your pelvic floor muscles are weakened from childbirth. Normally they can hold the urine in, but the violent pressure from your cough is physically forcing the valve open, causing the leakage. The constant wetness has chemically burned your skin. We must stop the cough with the prescribed syrup to stop the leakage, and apply a thick barrier cream to give your skin a chance to heal.
Actionable Steps: Take the cough syrup exactly as prescribed. Apply the barrier cream thickly after every pad change. Do not scrub the skin; pat it dry gently.

